# Placeholder audit — re-validation against current HEAD

Re-run 2026-08-20 against the **current** latest version of every template. The original
triage (2026-08-14) predates the secrets-audit cycle, so its findings were recorded against
versions that have since been superseded.

## Two headlines

**1. 31 templates → 8.** The secrets audit fixed most of this as a side effect. The original
document's "31 templates carrying genuine violations" is no longer true of the shipping
catalog. After triage, **6** are genuine.

**2. Five of those six need a DIFFERENT fix than this audit recommends.** That is the
finding that matters, because acting on the original recommendation would mean touching
five templates twice and leaving the real defect in place.

| | Original (2026-08-14) | Now (2026-08-20) |
|---|---|---|
| Templates with P1 violations | 31 | 8 |
| Genuine after triage | 60 findings | 6 templates |
| False positives | 8 | 2 |
| **Genuine, and a prefix fix is the right fix** | — | **1** |

## The 2 false positives

Placeholder text inside an **inline comment**, where the actual default is correct. A scanner
that skips whole-line comments still trips on these.

| Template | Line | Why it is fine |
|---|---|---|
| `cpln-trivy` 1.2.0 | `scope: "" # /subscriptions/<id>/...` | Value is `""`; the comment documents the Azure scope format |
| `mimir` 1.0.0 | `enabled: false # ... X-Scope-OrgID: <tenant>` | Value is `false`; the comment documents a header |

## Group A — 5 templates where the prefix is NOT the defect

The placeholder audit's remedy is "use the right prefix." For these five, a **later** ruling
supersedes it. CLAUDE.md's *"BUNDLED IS LOAD-BEARING"* (2026-08-14) and the
user-supplied-secrets convention both say the credential must not be a **value** at all —
either because it is a standalone datastore's product credential, or because it is
externally issued and would land in the Helm release in plaintext.

Renaming the placeholder here would make the value *look* handled while leaving the
credential exactly where the rulings say it must not be.

| Template | Current default | Why a prefix fix is wrong |
|---|---|---|
| `cassandra` 1.0.1 | `superuserPassword: supersecretpassword`, `password: password` | **Standalone datastore, working credential.** Install succeeds with a password published in our public repo |
| `clickhouse` 2.5.0 | `password: mypassword` | **Standalone datastore, working credential** |
| `kafka` 4.1.0 | `your-admin-password`, `your-user-password`, `your-inter-broker-password`, `your-kraft-cluster-id` | Standalone datastore; wrong prefix **and** wrong pattern |
| `ess` 2.0.0 | `token: <TOKEN>`, `accessKeyId: <ACCESS_KEY>`, `secretAccessKey: <SECRET_ACCESS_KEY>` | **Externally issued.** `essConfig` is dumped wholesale into a secret with `toYaml`, so a user's Vault token and AWS keys pass through Helm values and land in the release |
| `debezium-server` 1.1.1 | `password: "changeme"` for `db.example.com` | **Externally issued** — the user's own database credential, not bundled plumbing |

`cassandra`, `clickhouse` and `kafka` already appear on the outstanding standalone-datastore
list alongside `mongodb`, `postgis` and `timescaledb`. `ess` and `debezium-server` belong to
the same body of work for a different reason: third-party credentials taken as values.

## Group B — 1 template where the prefix fix IS correct

| Template | Current default | Correct form |
|---|---|---|
| `cdc-pipeline` 1.0.2 | `changeme-postgres-password`, `changeme-kafka-admin-password`, `changeme-kafka-debezium-password`, `changeme-kraft-cluster-id`, `changeme-inter-broker-password` | `change-me-cdc-pipeline-…` |

These are genuinely **bundled** plumbing: the chart bundles its own PostgreSQL and Kafka, no
human types the credentials elsewhere, so CLAUDE.md's internal-plumbing exception applies and
`change-me-` is right. The intent was already correct — the defect is that unprefixed
`changeme` is explicitly disallowed, and the rule wants `change-me-{service}-{purpose}`.

## Recommendation

Do `cdc-pipeline` as a placeholder-style fix. Route the other five into the
standalone-datastore / third-party-credential secrets work rather than handling them here.
